  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Each letter from $a$ to $j$ stands for a different number from 1 to 10. Is it possible for\n$$\\frac{a}{b}+\\frac{c}{d}+\\frac{e}{f}+\\frac{g}{h}+\\frac{i}{j}$$\nto be an integer?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Yes. For example, $\\frac15+\\frac8{10}+\\frac72+\\frac64+\\frac93=9$.\n\nEach number from 1 to 10 appears exactly once.",
      "worked": "Choose $(a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j)=(1,5,8,10,7,2,6,4,9,3)$. These are all ten distinct numbers from 1 to 10.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac15+\\frac8{10}+\\frac72+\\frac64+\\frac93&=\\frac2{10}+\\frac8{10}+\\frac{35}{10}+\\frac{15}{10}+\\frac{30}{10}\\\\\\frac15+\\frac8{10}+\\frac72+\\frac64+\\frac93&=\\frac{90}{10}\\\\\\frac15+\\frac8{10}+\\frac72+\\frac64+\\frac93&=9.\\end{align*}$$\nThus the sum can be an integer.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 54
  },
